n uses a hardcoded admin login string without any cautionary text. In markdown documentation, examples that use privileged credentials should clearly warn readers not to reuse them in production or copy them unchanged.","remediation":"Replace the hardcoded admin credential with placeholders and add a note explaining that production use should rely on securely managed, least-privilege credentials.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"references/doc_6182.md","start_line":493,"end_line":493}]},{"id":"SQP-2","finding_id":"finding-592a478d4cf94601acf8f4919f038263","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.92,"location":{"file":"references/doc_6228.md","start_line":13,"end_line":17},"finding":null,"explanation":"The document explains that strings are converted to metacode and then executed with eval, which is a safety-sensitive behavior because execution of constructed strings can affect system behavior or data. The markdown provides examples of direct evaluation but does not include any caution about trusting input sources or validating expressions first.","remediation":"Add a clear warning near the parseExpr/eval description and examples stating that evaluating untrusted or user-supplied strings can execute unintended logic, and recommend validating or restricting input before calling eval.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"references/doc_6228.md","start_line":13,"end_line":17}]},{"id":"SQP-2","finding_id":"finding-bde18c08deca4df7935a8d306d4ed631","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.92,"location":{"file":"references/doc_6361.md","start_line":13,"end_line":13},"finding":null,"explanation":"This markdown file describes a function that returns metadata for all chunks and the included example output reveals full local storage paths. Because markdown files should warn about beha